/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"note-level-0","number":0,"title":"Welcome","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"0.1","modules":[{"id":"note-m-0-1-101","code":"0.1","title":"0.1","blurb":"0.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l0-1-1","title":"0.1.1 — Note’s four operations: sketch, capture, sample, send","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.1 — Note’s four operations: sketch, capture, sample, send","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Name Note’s four jobs and open a Set that can hold a first clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note is a playable mobile app for iPhone and iPad. The opening of the official manual names the work you do here: sketch ideas anywhere, capture improvisations as clips, sample your environment, and send Sets onward. Those four verbs are the course spine. Everything later — pads, Capture, Cloud, export — is a way to finish one of those jobs."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sketching happens on the Session grid you see when a Set opens. Tracks run vertically. Empty clip slots sit under each track header. You add a clip, play pads, and Capture turns what you just played into a loop. Sampling is separate from Capture: you record the device microphone into a Drum Rack pad or a Sampler, or you import WAV, AIFF, M4A, or MP3. Sending is how the sketch leaves the phone. <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> uploads a Set to Live, Move, Push, or another Note install on the same Ableton account. <strong>Export Audio</strong> in <strong>Set Settings</strong> writes a WAV or M4A file without using Cloud. <strong>Share To…</strong> builds an <code>.ablbundle</code> that another Note install or Live 11.2.5+ can open."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note is not Live running on a phone. There is no Arrangement View. Cloud does not bring a Live Set back. You buy Note in the App Store; a Live license does not include it. The musical reason to keep the four verbs in mind is pacing: capture the idea while it is still in your hands, then decide later whether it belongs in Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Note and look at the Sets Library: <strong>+ New</strong>, Local Sets, Demo Sets, and the Cloud area if Cloud is on.","Tap <strong>+ New</strong>. Confirm three default tracks and an empty clip grid.","Find the back arrow left of the Set name (library) and the <strong>Set Settings</strong> icon to the right (tempo, metronome, key/scale, export).","Close the Set. Write the four verbs from memory: sketch, capture, sample, send."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What four operations does the Note manual assign to the app? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sketch ideas, capture improvisations, sample the environment, and send Sets (Cloud, export, or Share To…).","Does sending a Set require Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You can export audio from <strong>Set Settings</strong> or share an <code>.ablbundle</code> with <strong>Share To…</strong>.","Is Note a mobile copy of Live? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is a separate iOS/iPadOS app with a Session grid and no Arrangement View."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §1 Welcome to Note","Ableton Note Manual §3.1 Creating New Sets; §4 Session View","Ableton Note Manual §10 Transferring and Exporting Sets","Note FAQ, Getting Started with Note / Making Music with Note"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On paper, write one sentence for each verb that names the Note screen you will use later (Session grid, Capture, Sampler/Drum Rack, Cloud or Export). Keep the page. You use it as the Level 0 map."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-2","title":"0.1.2 — Note uses a Session grid, not Arrangement View","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.2 — Note uses a Session grid, not Arrangement View","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Point to tracks, clip slots, and scenes in an open Set and state what Note does not provide instead."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When you open a Set you land in Session View: tracks, clips, scenes, Set Settings, a mixer, and a play/stop button. Tracks sit vertically, the same orientation Live uses in its Session View. Under each track header is a column of clip slots. A new Set starts with three tracks and an empty grid of squares. You add a clip with the <strong>Add Clip</strong> icon; that jump opens Instrument View so you can play and Capture. Tap a captured clip and it launches on the nearest downbeat. A playing clip shows a <strong>Stop Clip</strong> icon."},{"type":"prose","html":"Scenes are the horizontal rows. Tap a scene number to launch every clip in that row together. <strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong> sits at the bottom of the grid next to <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong>, Undo/Redo, and global Play/Stop. You can play clips from different scenes at once, or fire a whole scene as a section. Long-press a track, clip, or scene number to move, duplicate, or delete it."},{"type":"prose","html":"There is no Arrangement View inside Note. You do not get a linear timeline, locator track, or arrange-edit pass on the phone. Linear arrangement and desktop mixing happen in Live after you send the Set. That is a design limit, not a hidden switch. The musical reason to care is how you think about form: in Note you stack alternative clips and scenes. You do not draw a song start-to-end on a ruler."},{"type":"prose","html":"As of Note 2.0 you can also record audio loops in Session, but the workspace is still the same grid. Do not look for an Arrangement page that is not there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set (or tap <strong>+ New</strong>).","Point to a vertical track, an empty clip slot, and the scene-number column. Toggle <strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong>.","Tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> on one track, then tap the back arrow left of the Set name to return to the grid.","Without looking at this page, name the three grid objects (track, clip slot, scene) and state the view Note does not have."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How are tracks and scenes arranged? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tracks vertical, scenes horizontal. A scene is one row of clips.","When does a tapped clip start? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the nearest downbeat.","Where do you do a linear timeline edit of a Note idea? <strong>Answer:</strong> In Ableton Live after you send the Set. Note has no Arrangement View."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View; §4.1 Tracks; §4.2 Clips; §4.3 Scenes; §4.5 Session View Actions","Ableton Note Manual §1 Welcome to Note","Note Release Notes 2.0.1, Audio Tracks &amp; Recording (Session remains the workspace)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open any Demo Set. Launch one clip, then launch a scene by its number. Write one sentence: “Note form is clips and scenes, not a timeline.” Keep that sentence in your notes for Level 2."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-3","title":"0.1.3 — Note and Live are separate products","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.3 — Note and Live are separate products","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"State what you buy when you buy Note, and what a Live license does and does not include."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note is an App Store purchase for iPhone and iPad. It is not bundled with any Ableton Live license. Owning Trial, Lite, Intro, Standard, Suite, or Education does not put Note on your phone. Owning Note does not turn the phone into Live and does not replace a full Live edition."},{"type":"prose","html":"You do not need an Ableton account to sketch, Capture, or sample on the device. You need an ableton.com account to turn on <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong>. You also do not need a Live license to use Note. The two products meet at the file, not at the store receipt: a Note Set opens in every Live edition from version 11.2.5 onward. A Set you save in Live cannot be opened back in Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"The practical split is money and workflow. Pay for Note once in the App Store (price is listed there in your currency; there are currently no in-app purchases). Use Note to start ideas. Use Live on the computer to arrange and finish. Cloud, <strong>Share To…</strong>, and <strong>Export Audio</strong> are the bridges. Treat “I already have Live, so I must already have Note” as false. Treat “I bought Note, so I own the full desktop DAW” as false."},{"type":"prose","html":"A Note purchase does include a claim path for Live 12 Lite if you do not already own Live. That serial is a separate desktop license. Lesson 0.1.4 covers how to claim it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the App Store listing for Ableton Note and confirm it is a paid app, not a Live add-on.","In Note, confirm you can create a Set without signing into Cloud.","Write two sentences: what a Live license does not include, and what a Note purchase does not replace.","Repeat those two sentences from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Does a Live license include Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Note is a separate App Store purchase.","Do you need a Live license to use Note on the device? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. An Ableton account is required only for Cloud.","Can a Live Set be opened in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Note Sets open in Live 11.2.5+; the path is one way."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Getting Started with Note (purchase, Live license, account, editions)","Note Compatibility (iOS 15+, Live 11.2.5+, Sets saved in Live cannot open in Note)","Ableton Note Manual §1.1 Setup; §2.1 Ableton Cloud; §10.1 (one-way sharing)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a four-line card: (1) buy Note in the App Store, (2) Live not included / Note not included with Live, (3) account needed only for Cloud, (4) Note Set → Live, never Live Set → Note. Keep the card with your version number later in 0.2.7."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-4","title":"0.1.4 — Live Lite that ships with Note","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.4 — Live Lite that ships with Note","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find the in-app claim path for Live 12 Lite and know which Live editions can open a Note Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Every Note purchase includes a copy of Live Lite. Ableton added Live 12 Lite for new and existing Note users in Note 1.1. You claim the serial inside the app: open <strong>App Settings</strong> and tap <strong>Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>. The FAQ path is the same: <strong>Settings → Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>. You receive a Lite 12 serial, register it on your Ableton account, then download and authorize Live 12 Lite on the computer."},{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ qualifies the offer: you get the free Live 12 Lite license if you do not already own an Ableton Live license. Lite is a desktop application. It is not Note with extra pages. Claiming it does not unlock AUv3, Arrangement View, or Live-to-Note Cloud inside the phone app."},{"type":"prose","html":"Independently of which edition you run, Note Sets open in all Live licenses and editions: Trial, Lite, Intro, Standard, Suite, and Education, from Live 11.2.5 onward. Cloud itself is available in those same editions at 11.2.5+. If you already work in Intro, Standard, or Suite, you still send Note Sets into that existing install. You do not need Lite in that case."},{"type":"prose","html":"Wavetable presets that originate in Note open in Live Standard, Lite, and Intro as a locked rack (macros only). Full Wavetable editing is a Suite/Pack feature on the Live side. That limit is a Live-side fact, not a Note-side instrument missing from the phone."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In the Sets Library, tap the three dots (bottom left) to open <strong>App Settings</strong>.","Locate <strong>Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>. If you do not already own Live, follow the prompts and write the serial in a password manager or Ableton account note. Do not post the serial.","On the computer, confirm Live is 11.2.5 or later before you plan to open Note Sets.","From memory, name the Settings entry and the Live version floor for opening Note Sets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you claim the Lite serial? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note <strong>App Settings → Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>.","Which Live editions open Note Sets? <strong>Answer:</strong> Trial, Lite, Intro, Standard, Suite, and Education, Live 11.2.5+.","If you already own Standard or Suite, must you install Lite to receive Note Sets? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Send the Set into the Live edition you already run (11.2.5+)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.8 Get Ableton Live Lite","Note FAQ, Getting Started (Live 12 Lite; editions that open Note Sets)","Note Compatibility (Live 11.2.5+; all editions)","Note Release Notes 1.1 (Live 12 Lite for new and existing Note users)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Add one line to the product card from 0.1.3: “Claim Lite at <strong>Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>; any Live 11.2.5+ edition opens Note Sets.” If you already own Live, write your edition and version instead of claiming a second license."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-5","title":"0.1.5 — Documented limits: 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips, 8 Cloud Sets","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.5 — Documented limits: 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips, 8 Cloud Sets","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Recite Note’s four official maxima and find the UI that hits each one."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton publishes four hard caps. A Note Set holds a maximum of 8 tracks. A Set holds a maximum of 8 scenes. A clip is a maximum of 16 bars long. Ableton Cloud holds a total of 8 Sets. Those numbers come from the Note FAQ and the manual. Do not replace them with forum guesses or with Live’s much larger track and scene counts."},{"type":"prose","html":"You add tracks by swiping left in Session View and tapping <strong>Add Track</strong>, up to eight. You add or duplicate scenes up to eight; long-press a scene number to rearrange, duplicate, or delete. Clip length is edited in the MIDI Editor loop brace; 16 bars is the stop. Cloud’s eight slots are transfer slots, not a backup drive. Synced Sets also remain on the device under Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → Projects."},{"type":"prose","html":"A Set may use fewer tracks, fewer scenes, and shorter clips. It cannot go past these maxima. Cloud used to hold 5 Sets; Note 1.2 raised the cap to 8. If a source you find still says 5, treat 8 as current."},{"type":"prose","html":"The musical reason to memorize the card is planning. Eight tracks is enough for drums, bass, harmony, lead, and a few extras — not a 40-track mix. Eight scenes is enough for intro, verse, chorus, and variations — not an infinite arrange. Sixteen bars is a section, not a full arrangement. Eight Cloud Sets means you rotate what sits in the pipe to Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set. Swipe left and count how many tracks you can add until <strong>Add Track</strong> stops at eight total.","Show scene numbers. Count the scene slots. Confirm the eighth is the last.","Open <strong>App Settings</strong> and note that Cloud is a toggle plus a library area, not unlimited storage.","Close the app. Write the four maxima from memory: 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips, 8 Cloud Sets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What are the four official maxima? <strong>Answer:</strong> 8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips, 8 Cloud Sets.","If you need a ninth track, what do you do? <strong>Answer:</strong> You cannot add it in Note. Finish or bounce a part, or continue in Live after send.","Does uploading a Set to Cloud remove the local copy? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The project also stays under Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → Projects."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Making Music with Note (max tracks, clip length, scenes)","Note FAQ, Using Ableton Cloud with Note (8 Sets; Cloud is not backup)","Ableton Note Manual §2.1 (store up to eight Sets); §4.1 (up to eight tracks); §4.3 (maximum of eight scenes)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (clips up to 16 bars); 1.2 (Cloud up to 8 Sets, was 5)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Copy the four-number card onto the same page as your four verbs. Next to each number write one musical consequence (for example: “8 scenes = plan sections now, arrange later”)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-6","title":"0.1.6 — Cloud transfers from Note to Live only","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.6 — Cloud transfers from Note to Live only","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"State the direction Cloud moves a Set and name the three places you turn Cloud on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton Cloud stores up to eight Sets so you can open them in Live’s browser and on other devices signed into the same Ableton account. In Note you enable it from three places: the <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> toggle in <strong>App Settings</strong>, the Enable Ableton Cloud banner in the Sets Library, or <strong>Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong> in a Set’s <strong>Set Options</strong> menu (three dots next to the play button). Turning the toggle on opens ableton.com so you can connect the service to your user account. Sign in with the same email as your Live license or the Set will not show in Live."},{"type":"prose","html":"Once uploaded, the Set appears in Live’s Cloud folder in Places, in the Cloud section of every Note install on that account, and — when those products are in the chain — on Move or Push. The local project remains on the device. Cloud is transfer and sync, not backup. If you need a copy for someone else, use <strong>Share To…</strong>. Cloud Sets are only visible to the person signed in with that account."},{"type":"prose","html":"The path is one way at the Live boundary. You can send a Note Set into Live. You cannot transfer a Live Set into Note. After you open a Note Set in Live and save it as a Live Set, that modified file no longer opens in Note. Always <strong>Collect All and Save</strong> in Live the first time you open Note content so samples stay with the Live Set if the Cloud slot is later reused."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note still works without Cloud. Export audio from <strong>Set Settings</strong>, or share an <code>.ablbundle</code> with <strong>Share To…</strong> / AirDrop."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings</strong> and find the <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> toggle. Do not upload a Set you care about until you confirm the account email.","In the Sets Library, find the Cloud banner and one Set’s <strong>Set Options → Upload to Ableton Cloud</strong>.","Write the one-way rule: Note → Live / Note / Move / Push. Not Live → Note.","Repeat the rule and the three enable points from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Can you send a Live Set back into Note via Cloud? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Live Sets cannot be opened in Note.","How many Sets does Cloud hold? <strong>Answer:</strong> 8. Rotate which Sets occupy the slots.","Why must the Ableton account email match the Live license? <strong>Answer:</strong> Cloud Sets appear only for that signed-in account. A mismatch is a common reason Sets do not show in Live."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud; §10.1 Transferring Sets using Ableton Cloud","Note FAQ, Using Ableton Cloud with Note (one-way; 8 Sets; not backup; same account)","Note Compatibility (optional Cloud; Sets saved in Live cannot open in Note)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Without uploading, list the three Cloud enable points and the one-way rule on your product card. Circle “not a backup.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-7","title":"0.1.7 — MIDI files cannot be imported or exported","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.7 — MIDI files cannot be imported or exported","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Separate MIDI file transfer (not available) from MIDI controller input and Capture (available)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ is direct: MIDI clips cannot currently be imported into or exported from Note. There is no <code>.mid</code> import, no MIDI clip export, and no drag of a Standard MIDI File into the Sets Library. If you arrive with a MIDI file from another app, Note has no door for it. If you want MIDI notes out of Note as a file, that door is also closed."},{"type":"prose","html":"Notes get into a clip by Capture. You play pads, Keys, or a class-compliant MIDI controller (USB or Bluetooth), then Capture writes what you played. Note can receive MIDI from a controller, from a Mac virtual MIDI network or Bluetooth, and from another app on the same device. Connected sources appear in <strong>App Settings → Link &amp; MIDI</strong>. That is live input, not file I/O. MIDI that arrives this way includes pitch, length, velocity, and aftertouch (including polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI as of Note 1.2.1). It is not MPE. Pitch bend is not available (Lesson 0.1.10). Sustain pedal is not a documented Note input."},{"type":"prose","html":"To move the music to another person or to Live, you send the Set: Cloud, <strong>Share To…</strong> (<code>.ablbundle</code>), or <strong>Export Audio</strong> (WAV 48 kHz 16-bit or M4A 48 kHz 32-bit). Live then sees Note’s clips, not a detached MIDI file."},{"type":"prose","html":"The musical reason to lock this now is so you do not plan a session around “I’ll just drop the MIDI in.” Sketch in Note, or play the part in. Finish file-level MIDI work in Live after the hand-off."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings → Link &amp; MIDI</strong> and confirm you see MIDI input toggles, not a MIDI file importer.","Open a Set, tap <strong>Add Clip</strong>, play a few pads. Notice Capture, not an Import MIDI control.","Check <strong>Set Options</strong>. Confirm <strong>Share To…</strong> and Cloud, not Export MIDI.","Write from memory: “No MIDI file in or out. Play or Capture. Send the Set.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Can you import or export MIDI clips in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The FAQ states MIDI clips cannot currently be imported or exported.","Can you still play Note from a MIDI keyboard? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Class-compliant USB or Bluetooth controllers send live MIDI in. That is not file import.","How do you get Note notes into Live? <strong>Answer:</strong> Upload the Set via Cloud, or share an <code>.ablbundle</code>, then open it in Live 11.2.5+."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Making Music with Note (no MIDI clip import/export; Capture; class-compliant controllers)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3 MIDI Input; §8.1 Capturing Clips; §10 Transferring and Exporting Sets","Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In one paragraph, explain to a future collaborator why you will send an <code>.ablbundle</code> or a Cloud Set instead of a <code>.mid</code> file. Keep the paragraph with your limits card."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-8","title":"0.1.8 — AUv3 is not available","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.8 — AUv3 is not available","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"State that Note does not host Audio Unit v3 devices and name the instruments you actually get."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note does not host AUv3 plug-ins. There is no Audio Unit browser, no third-party synth scan, and no Settings toggle that turns hosting on. The FAQ gives the reason: hosting AUv3 would prevent Sets from being compatible with Ableton Live on Windows. A Note Set has to open in Live on both macOS and Windows. An iOS-only plug-in inside the Set would break that promise."},{"type":"prose","html":"What you play instead are Note’s own devices, loaded as a <strong>Track Preset</strong> (instrument plus two audio effects). The manual lists four instruments: <strong>Drift</strong> (subtractive), <strong>Drum Rack</strong> (16 pads, each a Drum Sampler plus pad FX send and kit FX), <strong>Melodic Sampler</strong>, and <strong>Wavetable</strong>. Effects live in the two rightmost preset tabs. Those devices travel with the Set into Live. You do not get Suite-only Live devices as if they were installed on the phone."},{"type":"prose","html":"There are currently no in-app purchases that add AUv3. Full Drift parameter access is a separate, documented purchase path on the Drift icon; it expands Drift, it does not add plug-in hosting."},{"type":"prose","html":"Plan sounds inside Note’s parameter surface. Deeper synthesis theory belongs in the Sound Design sister course. Third-party desktop plug-ins belong in Live after you send the Set — and even then they are Live’s job, not Note’s."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set, tap a track header, and read the Track Preset name and its instrument icon.","Tap the preset name and scan categories. Confirm you see Note’s Core Library, not an AUv3 list.","Open <strong>App Settings</strong>. Confirm there is no AUv3 or plug-in host switch.","From memory, state the AUv3 rule and the reason (Live on Windows)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Can Note host AUv3? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Audio Unit v3 devices are not available in Note.","Why? <strong>Answer:</strong> Hosting AUv3 would break compatibility with Ableton Live on Windows.","What do you load on a track instead? <strong>Answer:</strong> A Track Preset: Drift, Drum Rack, Melodic Sampler, or Wavetable, plus two audio effects."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Making Music with Note (“Can Note host AUv3?”)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Track Presets (Drift, Drum Rack, Melodic Sampler, Wavetable)","Note FAQ, Getting Started (no in-app purchases)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write “No AUv3 — Sets must open in Live on Windows” on your limits card. Under it, list the four Note instruments from memory."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-9","title":"0.1.9 — No Android","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.9 — No Android","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Name the supported devices and OS versions, and stop looking for an Android build."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note is an iOS and iPadOS app. The FAQ states it is compatible with any iOS device running iOS 15 or higher, and that it is available for both iPhone and iPad. The manual tightens the floor to iOS 15.0 and iPadOS 15.0 or later. You buy it in the App Store."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note is not currently compatible with Android devices. There is no Play Store listing that Ableton documents, no sidecar APK, and no “Note for Android” setting. If a classmate works only on Android, they cannot run this app. They can still receive exported audio or, if they have Live 11.2.5+ on a computer, they can open an <code>.ablbundle</code> you share. They cannot run Note itself."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not treat a Windows or Android tablet as a Note machine. Do not plan a classroom around mixed phone OS unless every Android user will only listen to exports or work in Live on a computer. Cloud does not create an Android client. Link and Link Audio (Note 2.0+) still assume Note is running on an iPhone or iPad."},{"type":"prose","html":"This course is written for the device you can install: iPhone or iPad on 15.0+. If your device is below that OS, update iOS/iPadOS before Lesson 0.2.1, or use a supported device."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the iPhone or iPad, open Settings → General → About and write the iOS/iPadOS version.","Confirm the version is 15.0 or later.","Open the App Store, search Ableton Note, and confirm the listing is an Apple app, not an Android store page.","From memory, state the platform rule: iPhone and iPad, iOS/iPadOS 15.0+, not Android."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Does Note run on Android? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is not currently compatible with Android devices.","What is the minimum OS? <strong>Answer:</strong> iOS 15.0 and iPadOS 15.0 or later.","How can an Android-only collaborator hear your sketch? <strong>Answer:</strong> You export audio or share an <code>.ablbundle</code> they open in Live 11.2.5+ on a computer. They cannot install Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Getting Started with Note (iOS 15+, iPhone and iPad, not Android, App Store)","Ableton Note Manual §1.1 Setup (iOS 15.0 and iPadOS 15.0 or later; App Store)","Note Compatibility (iOS 15+; Live 11.2.5+ for opening Sets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the platform line on your product card: “iPhone/iPad · iOS/iPadOS 15.0+ · not Android.” If you teach or produce with Android users, add one line about export or Live as their only path."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-10","title":"0.1.10 — Pitch bend is not available","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.10 — Pitch bend is not available","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Confirm that Note has no pitch-bend control and name the MIDI you can still record."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ asks “Where is pitch bend for melodic instruments?” and answers that pitch bend is currently not available in Note. There is no bend wheel on the pad or Keys views, no documented pitch-bend lane in the MIDI Editor, and no Settings item that enables it. Until Ableton changes that FAQ line, treat bend as absent."},{"type":"prose","html":"You still play expressively inside what Note records. Capture stores notes you play on pads, Keys, or a class-compliant controller. MIDI input includes pitch (the note itself), length, velocity, and aftertouch, including polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI as of Note 1.2.1. Note 1.2.1 also documents Y-axis on Keys in In-Key mode for supported presets. None of that is pitch bend, MPE, or a sustain pedal. Do not invent a hidden bend gesture."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sampler and Drum Rack <strong>Transpose</strong> re-pitches a sample in semitones. That is a device parameter, not a performance bend wheel. On the Sampler/Drum Rack path the FAQ still states that transposing also changes playback speed because warping is not available on that path. Audio-track loops in Note 2.0.1 are a different rule (tempo and pitch independent). Do not mix those version-gated facts when you only needed “no bend.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"If a part requires wheel bends, record a simpler take in Note and add bend later in Live after you send the Set. Do not stall a sketch waiting for a control that is not in the app."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a melodic track. Look at pads and Keys. Confirm there is no bend wheel or bend strip.","Open <strong>App Settings → Link &amp; MIDI</strong>. Confirm mapping targets (macros, Capture, transport) and no Pitch Bend toggle.","Play a phrase with velocity changes and Capture it. That is the expression Note stores today.","Write from memory: “Pitch bend is not available. Capture pitch, length, velocity, aftertouch.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Is pitch bend available in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The FAQ states it is currently not available.","What MIDI does Note record from playing and controllers? <strong>Answer:</strong> Pitch, length, velocity, and aftertouch (including poly AT from external MIDI). Not MPE, not sustain, not bend.","Is Transpose the same as pitch bend? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Transpose is a Sampler/Drum Rack parameter in semitones, not a performance wheel."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Making Music with Note (“Where is pitch bend…?”)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3 MIDI Input; §2.3.5 MIDI Mapping","Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (polyphonic aftertouch; Y-axis on Keys in In-Key mode)","Note FAQ (Sampler/Drum Rack transpose vs warp) and RN 2.0.1 (audio-track warp — do not blend)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On your limits card add “No pitch bend.” Play a two-bar melody using only velocity and note length for shape. Listen back. That is the expression budget until Live."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-1-11","title":"0.1.11 — Related courses: topics included and excluded","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.1.11 — Related courses: topics included and excluded","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.1 — Note’s four verbs</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Draw the line between this Note course and the five sister courses so you do not expect the wrong curriculum here."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This course teaches Note’s documented mobile workflow: Session grid, Capture, pads, devices as they appear on the phone, samples, Link, MIDI into Note, Cloud, Share, and export. It also teaches a sketch mindset from <em>Beatmaking with Ableton Note</em>. It does not pretend Note is Live, and it does not replace musicianship or synthesis degrees."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sister-course boundaries from the syllabus:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Mastering Ableton Live 12</strong> owns the full desktop DAW: Arrangement View, desktop mixing, Live-only devices, and everything after Collect All and Save. This course only hands the Set over.","<strong>Drums and Rhythm Programming</strong> owns groove as a full musicianship course. Here you use Note’s Drum Rack, 16 pads, and mobile swing/groove controls. You do not complete a drum-programming degree on the phone.","<strong>Sound Design Foundations</strong> owns synthesis theory. Here you learn Note’s on-screen parameters for Drift, Wavetable, Sampler, and the two track effects. You do not treat a macro page as a full synth syllabus.","<strong>Foundations of Music Production</strong> owns full song architecture. Here you sketch sections as scenes (max 8) and leave long-form arrangement to Live or that course.","<strong>Learning the Launchkey MK4 61</strong> owns the desktop controller. Here you only cover MIDI into Note (class-compliant USB or Bluetooth). Hardware programming beyond that input path is the Launchkey course."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not import those courses’ product claims into Note lessons. If a control is not in the Note manual, FAQ, Knowledge Base, or release notes, it is out of scope. Android, AUv3, MIDI file I/O, Live→Note Cloud, and pitch bend stay on the “Note will not” list."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Copy the five sister-course names into your notes.","Next to each, write one phrase this Note course will teach and one phrase it refuses.","Re-read your 0.1.5 limits card. Check that none of your “Note will” lines break those maxima.","Recite the split for Live and for Launchkey from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you learn Arrangement View? <strong>Answer:</strong> In Mastering Ableton Live 12 (and in Live after you send the Set). Not inside Note.","What does this course cover on a Launchkey? <strong>Answer:</strong> MIDI into Note only. The full controller course is Learning the Launchkey MK4 61.","What synthesis does Note class teach? <strong>Answer:</strong> The mobile parameter surface on Drift, Wavetable, Sampler, and Drum Rack — not Sound Design Foundations theory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Mobile Production Course Syllabus § sister courses / §6 Sister-course boundaries","Ableton Note Manual §1 Welcome; §4 Session View","Note FAQ (limits; no AUv3; no MIDI file I/O; no Android; no pitch bend; one-way Cloud)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a 20-second elevator pitch: what Note is (four verbs, Session grid) and what this course will not teach (full Live, full drums degree, full synth theory, full song architecture, full Launchkey). Speak it once without notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-1-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-0-2-102","code":"0.2","title":"0.2","blurb":"0.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l0-2-1","title":"0.2.1 — App Store install; iOS/iPadOS 15+","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.1 — App Store install; iOS/iPadOS 15+","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Install Note from the App Store on a device that meets the OS floor."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note is sold only in Apple’s App Store. The FAQ: purchase Note in the App Store to install it on your iOS device. The manual: Note is available to purchase from the App Store and is supported on iOS 15.0 and iPadOS 15.0 or later. iPhone and iPad both count. Android does not."},{"type":"prose","html":"Before you pay, open Settings → General → About and read the iOS or iPadOS version. If it is below 15.0, update the system or use another device. The App Store listing shows the price in your local currency. There are currently no in-app purchases in Note. A Live license does not include the app; you still buy Note separately."},{"type":"prose","html":"After install, the Sets Library is the first screen: <strong>+ New</strong>, Demo Sets, Local Sets, and later a Cloud area. You can make music with no Ableton account. Create the account when you are ready for Cloud, not as a blocker for this lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you already installed Note, you still do this check: confirm the store source (App Store, not a sideload) and the OS version. Sideloading is out of scope for this course."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write your device type (iPhone or iPad) and the iOS/iPadOS version from Settings → General → About.","Open the App Store, search <strong>Ableton Note</strong>, and install or confirm it is installed.","Launch Note once and leave it on the Sets Library.","From memory, state the store and the OS floor."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you buy Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> The App Store. It is not included with a Live license.","What OS versions are supported? <strong>Answer:</strong> iOS 15.0 and iPadOS 15.0 or later, on iPhone and iPad.","Are there in-app purchases? <strong>Answer:</strong> There are currently no in-app purchases in Note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §1.1 Setup","Note FAQ, Getting Started with Note (App Store, iOS 15+, iPhone and iPad, not with Live, no IAP)","Note Compatibility (iOS 15+)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Screenshot the App Store listing (price visible) and your iOS version. Keep both private in your course notes. You need the OS number again if a lesson is version-gated."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-2","title":"0.2.2 — First-launch microphone access","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.2 — First-launch microphone access","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Grant microphone access when Note asks so you can record samples later."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The first time you launch Note, the app requests microphone access so you can record samples. That prompt is expected. Sampling your environment is one of the four verbs. Without the mic, you can still play instruments and Capture MIDI, but you cannot record a new audio file into a Drum Rack pad or Sampler from the device microphone."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tap Allow when iOS shows the permission dialog. If you dismiss it, Note will not see the mic until you grant access later (Lesson 0.2.3). The FAQ also notes that Note accesses the microphone by default, which is why an orange microphone indicator appears in iOS while the app is in use. That icon is expected, not a bug (Lesson 0.3.5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"You do not need Cloud or a Live license for this permission. You do need it before any sample-record lesson. External audio interfaces appear later in Note 2.0.1 as selectable inputs; the first-launch request is still the system microphone permission."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you are in a shared space, you can grant access now and wait to record until you have a quiet moment. The grant is the setup step; the recording is later."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Launch Note. If the microphone dialog appears, tap Allow.","If you already launched Note earlier, skip to Lesson 0.2.3 to verify the toggle instead of waiting for a dialog that will not return.","Leave the app in the foreground and glance at the status indicators. An orange mic dot is normal.","Write from memory: first launch asks for the mic so you can record samples."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why does Note ask for the microphone on first launch? <strong>Answer:</strong> To record samples. The manual states this is the first-launch request.","Can you Capture pad performances without the mic? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Capture records MIDI you play. The mic is for audio sampling.","Is the orange microphone indicator a problem? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Note accesses the mic by default; the icon is expected."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §1.1 Setup (first-launch microphone access)","Note FAQ, Reporting Problems (orange microphone icon is expected)","Ableton Note Manual §9 Using Samples (record into Drum Rack or Sampler)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In your setup notes, tick “Mic permission: granted / not yet.” If not yet, do 0.2.3 immediately after this lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-3","title":"0.2.3 — Re-grant mic: iOS Settings → Privacy → Microphone","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.3 — Re-grant mic: iOS Settings → Privacy → Microphone","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn the system microphone toggle on if you denied it at first launch."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you tapped Don’t Allow, or if sampling later fails silently, you re-grant access in iOS, not inside a hidden Note menu. The manual path is your device’s <strong>Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Microphone</strong>. Find <strong>Note</strong> in the app list and enable the toggle."},{"type":"prose","html":"The same idea appears as <strong>Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Microphone</strong> on current iOS wording. Older shorthand is Settings → Privacy → Microphone. Use whichever labels your OS shows; the destination is the system microphone list, not Note’s <strong>App Settings</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"After you flip the toggle, return to Note. You do not need to reinstall the app. If Note was backgrounded, bring it to the foreground so it rereads the permission. Then, when you later tap <strong>Record Sample</strong> on a Drum Rack pad or Sampler, the input should be live."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep this path next to the Cloud cellular-data path (Lesson 0.2.4). Both are iOS Settings for the Note app, but they live in different places: microphone under Privacy &amp; Security, cellular under the Note app’s settings page."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Leave Note. Open the iOS <strong>Settings</strong> app.","Go to <strong>Privacy &amp; Security → Microphone</strong>.","Enable <strong>Note</strong>. If it is already on, leave it on and write “already granted.”","Reopen Note. From memory, recite the path without looking at this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you re-grant the microphone? <strong>Answer:</strong> iOS Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Microphone → Note.","Is that toggle inside Note’s three-dots <strong>App Settings</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is a system privacy switch.","Do you need to delete and reinstall Note after denying the prompt? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Flip the Microphone toggle and return to the app."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §1.1 Setup (grant access via Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Microphone)","Note FAQ, Making Music with Note (audio recorded into Sampler or Drum Rack)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Screenshot the Microphone list with Note enabled (cover other apps if you want). File it with the 0.2.1 OS screenshot."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-4","title":"0.2.4 — Cellular data toggle for Cloud (Wi-Fi-only mode)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.4 — Cellular data toggle for Cloud (Wi-Fi-only mode)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Decide whether Cloud may use cellular data, and set the iOS toggle to match."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note can upload Sets over Wi-Fi or, if you allow it, over mobile/cellular data. The manual tells you how to lock Cloud to Wi-Fi: open the device <strong>Settings → Apps → Note</strong> and switch off the <strong>Mobile/Cellular Data</strong> toggle. Cloud then transfers Sets only when Wi-Fi is enabled."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note needs an internet connection to upload. If mobile/cellular data and Wi-Fi are both off, uploads wait and start automatically once a connection is available. That queue is useful on a plane; it is also how a Set can leave the device later than you expected if you leave cellular on."},{"type":"prose","html":"This toggle is not the <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> switch inside Note. The in-app toggle authorizes the service and your Ableton account. The iOS cellular toggle only controls whether the radio may carry those uploads. You can keep Cloud enabled and still refuse cellular."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use Wi-Fi-only mode if your plan is metered or if you upload Sets with large samples (Cloud warns around 100 MB of sample data). Use cellular if you need the Set in Live before you reach Wi-Fi and you accept the data use."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open iOS <strong>Settings → Apps → Note</strong> (or the Note row in Settings, depending on OS layout).","Find <strong>Mobile Data</strong> / <strong>Cellular Data</strong>. Set it off for Wi-Fi-only Cloud, or on if you accept cellular uploads.","Write which state you chose and why, in one sentence.","Recite the path from memory: device Settings → Note → Mobile/Cellular Data."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you stop Cloud from using cellular data? <strong>Answer:</strong> Device Settings → Apps → Note, turn off <strong>Mobile/Cellular Data</strong>. Cloud then uses Wi-Fi only.","What happens if there is no connection when you upload? <strong>Answer:</strong> Uploads begin automatically once a connection is available.","Does turning cellular off disable Cloud itself? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Cloud stays authorized; it simply waits for Wi-Fi."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §1.1 Setup (Mobile/Cellular Data toggle)","Ableton Note Manual §2.1 Ableton Cloud (internet required; uploads wait for a connection)","Note Release Notes 1.0.6 (100 MB Cloud sample warning)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Label your product card “Cloud radio: Wi-Fi only” or “Cloud radio: Wi-Fi + cellular.” Leave the in-app Cloud toggle for Level 1 unless you already use it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-5","title":"0.2.5 — Open App Settings (three dots, Set Library)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.5 — Open App Settings (three dots, Set Library)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Note’s App Settings from the Sets Library and name the pages you will use next."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"App Settings is Note’s global panel. It is not <strong>Set Settings</strong> (the icon to the right of an open Set’s name). You open App Settings from the Sets Library: tap the <strong>three dots</strong> in the bottom-left corner. That control is the App Settings icon."},{"type":"prose","html":"Inside you enable <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> and <strong>Link</strong>, set up MIDI input sources, configure <strong>Preferences</strong> (themes, Capture Timeout, pad names, melodic pad layout, <strong>Show Touches</strong>, and — on pre-2.0 builds — <strong>Monitor Audio Input</strong>), and open <strong>Help</strong>. You also reach <strong>Privacy &amp; Legal</strong>, <strong>Leave a Review</strong>, <strong>Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>, and <strong>Join Ableton Discord</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Memorize the two settings doors now so later lessons can say “open App Settings” without a hunt:"},{"type":"list","items":["Library → bottom-left <strong>three dots</strong> = App Settings (the app).","Open Set → icon to the right of the Set name = Set Settings (tempo, metronome, key/scale, Groove Amount, export)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"If you are inside a Set, tap the back arrow left of the Set name to return to the library first."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["If a Set is open, tap the back arrow to the Sets Library.","Tap the <strong>three dots</strong> at the bottom left.","Scroll the list once. Do not change anything yet. Note the entries for Cloud, Link &amp; MIDI, Preferences, Help, Privacy &amp; Legal, Review, Live Lite, Discord.","Close App Settings. Open them again from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is App Settings? <strong>Answer:</strong> Three dots, bottom-left of the Sets Library.","How is that different from Set Settings? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Settings is the icon to the right of the Set name inside an open Set.","Name three things App Settings contains. <strong>Answer:</strong> Any three of: Ableton Cloud, Link &amp; MIDI, Preferences, Help, Privacy &amp; Legal, Leave a Review, Get Ableton Live Lite, Join Ableton Discord."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2 App Settings; §2.1–2.9","Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View (back arrow; Set Settings icon)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Draw a two-box map: Library three-dots vs in-Set gear. Put Cloud/Help/version in the first box and tempo/export in the second. That map stays in your notes through Level 3."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-6","title":"0.2.6 — Help: videos, manual, KB, release notes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.6 — Help: videos, manual, KB, release notes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Help and locate the official learning links you will trust over random videos."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ’s answer to “Where do I learn how to make music with Note?” is: watch the Learn Note video, read the Note manual, and check the Ableton Knowledge Base. Those same materials live inside the app. <strong>App Settings → Help</strong> contains links to learning resources such as videos, the manual, and Knowledge Base articles, as well as the release notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use that page as your source list for the rest of the course. The local extracts you study here match those official documents. When a lesson is version-gated (audio tracks, Link Audio, monitoring location), you confirm the fact in Help’s release notes, not on a social post."},{"type":"prose","html":"Help also holds the installed version number (next lesson), plus paths to send feedback, report bugs, join user research, or join the Note beta. Those are separate actions. This lesson is only: find the documents."},{"type":"prose","html":"Languages: Note is available in English, German, French, Japanese, and Chinese. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not state whether Help always opens the English manual regardless of UI language. Use the language the app gives you and match it to the extract when you can."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings</strong> (library, bottom-left three dots) and tap <strong>Help</strong>.","Open each official link once: videos, manual, Knowledge Base, release notes. Confirm they load.","Bookmark or leave the release-notes page available. You will need it in 0.2.7 and in every 2.x lesson.","Close Help. Recite the four official places: video, manual, Knowledge Base, release notes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you open Help? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings → Help</strong>.","What does Ableton tell you to use to learn Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> The Learn Note video, the Note manual, and the Knowledge Base. Release notes document updates.","Where are release notes listed if you are not in the app? <strong>Answer:</strong> https://www.ableton.com/en/release-notes/note/ (FAQ)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.5 Help","Note FAQ, Making Music with Note (“Where do I learn…”)","Note FAQ, Reporting Problems (“Where can I find release notes…”)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a one-line source policy in your notes: “If it is not in the manual, FAQ, KB, or release notes, I do not teach it as a Note fact.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-7","title":"0.2.7 — Record the installed Note version","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.7 — Record the installed Note version","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the installed Note version on a card you keep for every later version-gated lesson."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ: to check what version of Note you are running, go to <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. The manual: the Help page displays the version number of your current Note installation, which is useful to include with bug reports."},{"type":"prose","html":"Write that number down. This course treats the printed manual as the pre-2.0 surface. Note 2.0 / 2.0.1 facts (audio tracks, warped audio loops, Link Audio, Auto Shift, Erosion, monitoring moved into the track, Beat/Synth/Audio add-track buttons, piano-layout default, C Major default) come from the release notes. If your Help page shows 1.x, skip 2.x procedures or treat them as reading only. If it shows 2.0 or 2.0.1+, use the 2.x lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"After an App Store update, open Help again and rewrite the number. Auto-update (Lesson 0.2.15) can change the app under you. A wrong version is how people invent features that are not on their device, or miss features that are."},{"type":"prose","html":"Include the version in any bug report (Lesson 0.2.8). Ableton asks for reproducible steps; the version is part of those steps."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings → Help</strong>.","Read the version number. Write it on a physical sticky or the first page of your notes: “Note version ____.”","Next to it, write “manual = pre-2.0 surface; 2.x = release notes.”","Close Help. Recite your version without looking."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the version number? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings → Help</strong>.","Why does this course care? <strong>Answer:</strong> 2.0/2.0.1 features are documented in the release notes, not in the pre-2.0 manual. Your version decides which lessons apply.","What do you do after an App Store update? <strong>Answer:</strong> Open Help again and rewrite the version."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.5 Help (version number)","Note FAQ, The Note App (“How do I check what version…”)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (version-gated feature list)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Photograph or copy the Help page so the version is readable. Keep it with the 0.2.1 OS screenshot. That pair is your compatibility card."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-8","title":"0.2.8 — Submit feedback / bug reports / beta join path","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.8 — Submit feedback / bug reports / beta join path","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use Help to send feedback, file a reproducible bug, or join the documented beta/research paths."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>App Settings → Help</strong> is also the support desk. The manual: you can submit bug reports, send feedback about your experience, sign up for user research testing, or join the Note beta to try features before they are released. The FAQ splits the jobs."},{"type":"prose","html":"Feature request or general suggestion: from <strong>App Settings</strong>, tap <strong>Help → Feedback</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Reproducible bug: list the exact steps that trigger the problem and submit that list using Ableton’s contact form. You must be logged in to your Ableton account to contact Technical Support. Include the version from Help (Lesson 0.2.7), device type, and iOS/iPadOS version. For crashes you can reproduce, Ableton’s screen-recording article tells you to add Screen Recording to Control Center, enable <strong>Show Touches</strong>, and capture the steps to share with Support."},{"type":"prose","html":"Crash that already happened: the FAQ says a crash report is sent automatically to Ableton’s developers. You do not need to contact Support for that automatic report. Privacy &amp; Legal still lets you manage crash-reporting options (Lesson 0.2.9)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Refunds are not this page. Ableton cannot directly refund the App Store purchase; you apply in the App Store."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts name “join the Note beta” and “sign up for user research testing” as Help actions but do not document a TestFlight URL or the exact on-screen field names after you tap those entries. Follow the in-app labels."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings → Help</strong>.","Find <strong>Feedback</strong>, the bug-report / contact path, user research, and Note beta. Open Feedback only if you have something to send; otherwise note the labels and leave.","Write a dummy bug in your notes (do not send it): three numbered steps, expected result, actual result, version, device, OS.","Recite: Feedback for ideas; contact form (logged in) for reproducible bugs; crashes auto-report."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you send a feature request? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings → Help → Feedback</strong>.","What must a bug report include? <strong>Answer:</strong> Exact steps to trigger the issue, submitted via the contact form while logged into an Ableton account. Add version, device, and OS.","Must you email Support after every crash? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. A crash report is sent automatically. Contact Support when you have a reproducible issue."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.5 Help","Note FAQ, Reporting Problems (crash auto-report; contact form; Feedback; release notes)","How to Create a Screen Recording in Note (reproducible crashes / performances)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save a blank bug template in your notes: Steps / Expected / Actual / Note version / iOS version / wired or Bluetooth. Fill it the next time something breaks instead of writing “it doesn’t work.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-9","title":"0.2.9 — Privacy & Legal; crash reporting; usage data + Cloud sync of consent","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.9 — Privacy & Legal; crash reporting; usage data + Cloud sync of consent","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Privacy &amp; Legal, see the crash and usage controls, and know that Cloud can sync consent."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>App Settings → Privacy &amp; Legal</strong> holds the legal documents and the data switches. The manual: this page includes options for managing your user data and enabling crash reporting. You can also view the End User License Agreement, Third Party Licenses, and Privacy Policy."},{"type":"prose","html":"Crash reporting ties to the FAQ: if Note crashes, a report is sent automatically to Ableton’s developers, who log it. You do not need to contact Support for that automatic report. Still open this page so you know the switch exists and can set it to match how you want to share diagnostics."},{"type":"prose","html":"When <strong>Ableton Cloud</strong> is enabled, usage-data consent is synced across all instances of Note. Note 1.0.15 states the same: consent is synced across Note instances when you are logged into Cloud. Turning the choice on one phone can therefore show up on another device signed into the same account. Read the switch before you assume each install is isolated."},{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ on data protection points you to the EULA, General Terms and Conditions, and the Ableton Privacy Policy. Ableton may process information from your use of Cloud under applicable data-protection rules. Ableton does not claim ownership of the intellectual property in your Cloud Sets; you retain the rights you already hold. Details sit in the Ableton Cloud Terms of Service."},{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson is not legal advice. It is the map to the screens and the Cloud-consent fact."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings → Privacy &amp; Legal</strong>.","Open EULA, Third Party Licenses, and Privacy Policy far enough to see they load. Set crash reporting and usage-data options deliberately.","If Cloud is on, write “consent syncs across Note instances on this account.”","Close the page. Recite what lives here: documents, crash reporting, usage data, Cloud-synced consent."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you manage crash reporting and usage data in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings → Privacy &amp; Legal</strong>.","What happens to usage-data consent when Cloud is enabled? <strong>Answer:</strong> Consent syncs across all instances of Note on that account.","Does Ableton own the content of your Cloud Sets? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The FAQ states you retain the intellectual property rights you hold in your sets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.6 Privacy &amp; Legal","Note FAQ, Reporting Problems (automatic crash reports)","Note FAQ, My Account (Cloud IP; data protection)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (consent synced across Note instances)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write one line in your notes: crash reporting on/off, usage data on/off, Cloud on/off. That line is the privacy state you assume for the rest of Level 0."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-10","title":"0.2.10 — Themes: Dark vs High Contrast (AAA)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.10 — Themes: Dark vs High Contrast (AAA)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Switch between Dark and High Contrast and know why High Contrast exists."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>App Settings → Preferences → Themes</strong> offers two UI themes: <strong>Dark</strong> and <strong>High Contrast</strong>. That is the full documented set. There is no separate “Light” theme in the manual."},{"type":"prose","html":"High Contrast arrived in Note 1.0.12 as an AAA High Contrast theme. AAA is the WCAG contrast grade Ableton cites for that theme. Use it when you need stronger separation between pads, clip slots, and text — outdoor glare, low vision, or a projector. Dark is the other documented look."},{"type":"prose","html":"Theme is a preference, not a Set setting. Changing it does not alter tempo, samples, or Cloud. It applies to the app UI. Accessibility work continues in later release notes (VoiceOver on visual controls in 2.0.1; direct-touch changes). This lesson is only the two themes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pick one now and work in it for the rest of Level 0 so screenshots stay consistent. You can switch later when you record tutorials (pair with <strong>Show Touches</strong> in 0.2.11)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings → Preferences</strong>.","Open <strong>Themes</strong>. Select <strong>High Contrast</strong>, return to the Sets Library, and look at contrast on Set tiles and buttons.","Switch to <strong>Dark</strong> and compare. Leave the theme you will use for study.","From memory, name both themes and the AAA label on High Contrast."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many themes does the manual document? <strong>Answer:</strong> Two: Dark and High Contrast.","What does AAA refer to? <strong>Answer:</strong> High Contrast is the AAA theme introduced in Note 1.0.12.","Does the theme live in Set Settings? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is an App Settings preference."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.1 Themes","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (AAA High Contrast theme)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Stay on one theme for a 10-minute browse of a Demo Set. If pads or scene numbers are hard to read, switch to High Contrast and leave it there."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-11","title":"0.2.11 — Show Touches for tutorials/screen recordings","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.11 — Show Touches for tutorials/screen recordings","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn on Show Touches before you record the screen so taps are visible."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Show Touches</strong> lives in <strong>App Settings → Preferences → Other</strong>. Enable it and a small white circle follows your finger across the UI. The manual’s reason is performance videos and tutorial content. Note 1.0.12 moved that visualization into Preferences (it had lived elsewhere earlier)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton’s screen-recording article uses the same switch. For a reproducible crash, or to record a performance to send to Support, add Screen Recording to iOS Control Center (follow Apple’s instructions), enable <strong>Access Within Apps</strong>, then enable <strong>Show Touches</strong> in Note. Open Control Center while Note is in front and start the system recording. The white circle is what makes the video readable."},{"type":"prose","html":"Show Touches does not change audio, latency, or Capture. You can leave it on while you practice if the circle helps you see your own gestures. Turn it off when it gets in the way of playing pads."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not Note recording the screen itself. Note has no documented in-app movie renderer. You use iOS Screen Recording plus this overlay."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings → Preferences → Other</strong>.","Enable <strong>Show Touches</strong>. Return to the Sets Library and tap around until you see the white circle.","If you will file a bug later, add Screen Recording to Control Center now (iOS Settings → Control Center) so the control is there when Note is open.","Recite the path: Preferences → Other → Show Touches, plus iOS Control Center for the actual video."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Show Touches draw? <strong>Answer:</strong> A small white circle that follows your gestures.","Where is the toggle? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings → Preferences → Other</strong> (moved there in 1.0.12).","Does enabling Show Touches record the screen? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You still start iOS Screen Recording from Control Center."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.5.1 Show Touches","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (touch visualization moved to Preferences)","How to Create a Screen Recording in Note"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With Show Touches on, record five seconds of opening a Demo Set (or skip the record if you cannot add Control Center right now, and just watch the circle). Stop. Confirm the circle is the only extra graphic Note added."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-12","title":"0.2.12 — Claim Live Lite serial","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.12 — Claim Live Lite serial","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Claim the Live 12 Lite serial from App Settings if you do not already own Live."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Lesson 0.1.4 explained that Note includes Live 12 Lite. This lesson is the click path. In <strong>App Settings</strong>, tap <strong>Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>. The FAQ: you receive a Lite 12 serial number you can register in your Ableton account, then download and authorize Live 12 Lite. Note 1.1 added this Settings link for new and existing Note users."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you already own a Live license, the FAQ’s offer is “if you don’t already own an Ableton Live license.” Do not fight the store over a second Lite serial you will not use. You still open Note Sets in the edition you already have, from Live 11.2.5 up."},{"type":"prose","html":"The serial is not a Note unlock. It does not add Arrangement View, AUv3, or Live→Note Cloud to the phone. It is a desktop license. Register it on ableton.com, download Live 12 Lite for your computer, and authorize there."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the serial private. Ableton account email for this license should be the same email you will use for Cloud, so later uploads land in the Live browser you actually open."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings</strong> and tap <strong>Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>.","If you do not own Live, follow the prompts, register the serial on your Ableton account, and store it in a password manager.","If you already own Live, write your edition and version instead, and confirm it is 11.2.5+.","From memory, name the Settings entry and say what product the serial authorizes (desktop Live 12 Lite)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the claim control? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings → Get Ableton Live Lite</strong>.","What do you receive? <strong>Answer:</strong> A Live 12 Lite serial to register, download, and authorize on the computer.","Does claiming Lite change Note’s feature set? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Lite is a separate desktop application."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.8 Get Ableton Live Lite","Note FAQ, Getting Started (how to activate Live 12 Lite)","Note Release Notes 1.1 (Settings link; Lite for new and existing users)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On your compatibility card, write either the last four characters of the serial (not the whole key) plus “registered,” or “already own [edition] [version].”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-12</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-13","title":"0.2.13 — Join Ableton Discord link","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.13 — Join Ableton Discord link","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find the in-app Discord link and treat it as a community door, not a second manual."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>App Settings</strong> includes <strong>Join Ableton Discord</strong>. Tapping it takes you to the Ableton Discord, where you can connect with other music makers. The link was added in Note 1.2."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the entire documented behavior. The extract does not name channels, roles, or moderation rules. {type:'gap'} Official channel names and whether a Note-specific room exists are not in the manual, FAQ, or release notes. After you join, read whatever rules Discord and Ableton show on screen."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not replace Help, the manual, or release notes with Discord advice. Community tips invent Android ports, AUv3 hosting, MIDI file export, and Live→Note Cloud. This course’s source policy still holds: product facts come from the official extracts."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use Discord if you want peers. Use <strong>Help → Feedback</strong> for feature requests and the contact form for bugs. Those paths reach Ableton; a Discord thread may not."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings</strong> and locate <strong>Join Ableton Discord</strong>.","Tap it only if you want to join. Create or use a Discord account if the site asks.","If you join, mute noisy channels and pin this course’s source policy in your own notes.","Recite where the link lives and what it is for (community, not official documentation)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the Discord control? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings → Join Ableton Discord</strong>.","When did the link appear? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.2.","Is a Discord answer an official Note fact? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Confirm facts in the manual, FAQ, Knowledge Base, or release notes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.9 Join Ableton Discord","Note Release Notes 1.2 (Join Discord link in app settings)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Decide yes/no on joining. Write that decision on your setup card. If yes, do not change any Note preference based on a Discord post until you can cite a source from 0.2.6."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-13</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-14","title":"0.2.14 — Leave a Review (optional)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.14 — Leave a Review (optional)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Know the two documented paths to an App Store review and that the review is optional."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Leave a Review</strong> sits in <strong>App Settings</strong>. The manual: use this option to share your feedback about Note in the App Store. Note 1.0.6 added the entry. The FAQ gives the store-side path: select Ableton Note in the App Store, under Ratings and Reviews pick a star rating, then tap <strong>Write a review</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson is optional. A review does not unlock features. There are currently no in-app purchases. A review is also not a bug report. Bugs go through <strong>Help</strong> and the contact form (Lesson 0.2.8). Feature ideas go to <strong>Help → Feedback</strong>. Mixing a crash log into a one-star review does not create a ticket."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you write a review, state the Note version from Help and whether you are on iPhone or iPad. That is the only product detail this course asks you to include. Do not paste serial numbers."},{"type":"prose","html":"Skip the review entirely if you have not used Capture yet. You will know more after Level 5."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>App Settings</strong> and find <strong>Leave a Review</strong>. Do not tap it unless you intend to review now.","Optionally open the App Store listing and locate <strong>Ratings and Reviews → Write a review</strong>.","If you review, include your Note version. If you skip, write “review deferred” on your setup card.","Recite the two paths and the rule: reviews are optional and are not bug tickets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where does Note send you to leave a review? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings → Leave a Review</strong>, which shares feedback in the App Store. You can also write a review on the App Store listing.","Is a review required to use Cloud or Lite? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is optional and unlocks nothing.","Where do bugs go instead? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Help</strong> plus Ableton’s contact form, with reproducible steps."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.7 Leave a Review","Note FAQ, The Note App (“How do I post a review in the App Store?”)","Note Release Notes 1.0.6 (Leave a Review in App Settings)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Choose review now, review later, or never. Write the choice in one line. If you write later, attach it to “after my first exported sketch.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-14</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-2-15","title":"0.2.15 — Auto-update via App Store settings","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.2.15 — Auto-update via App Store settings","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.2 — Install, permissions, Help, version</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set App Store auto-update on or off on purpose, then re-read the Note version after every update."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ: if App Updates are turned on in <strong>iOS Settings → App Store</strong>, Note will automatically update. That switch is an iOS setting, not a Note preference. Note does not document a separate in-app updater."},{"type":"prose","html":"Automatic updates are how 1.x devices become 2.x devices without a classroom announcement. That is useful (bug fixes, new devices such as Auto Shift and Erosion in 2.0.1) and disruptive (monitoring moves out of Settings in 2.0.1; audio-track rules appear). This course version-gates those facts. If auto-update is on, your job after any update is Lesson 0.2.7 again: <strong>App Settings → Help</strong>, rewrite the version, skim the release notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you need a frozen version for a project or a class, turn App Updates off in the App Store settings and update Note manually when you choose. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not document a way to install a specific older Note build from the App Store. You can only choose automatic versus manual updates of whatever the store currently serves."},{"type":"prose","html":"There are currently no in-app purchases, so an update is not a storefront upsell inside Note. Still read the 2.0.1 notes before you assume Sampler transpose rules apply to new audio tracks."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open iOS <strong>Settings → App Store</strong> and find App Updates.","Set it on (Note updates itself) or off (you update from the App Store page). Write the choice.","Open Note <strong>Help</strong> and confirm the version still matches your sticky.","Recite: auto-update lives in iOS App Store settings; after any update, reread Help."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What makes Note update by itself? <strong>Answer:</strong> App Updates turned on in iOS Settings → App Store.","Where do you confirm what you now have? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note <strong>App Settings → Help</strong>.","Can this course guarantee you can roll back to a listed old version? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. {type:'gap'} Official extracts do not document installing a specific older Note build."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, The Note App (“Is Note automatically updated?”)","Ableton Note Manual §2.5 Help (version number)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (example of a behavior-changing update)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Add “App Updates: on/off” to the compatibility card. Put a reminder: after any update, rewrite the version before you continue a version-gated lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-2-15</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-0-3-103","code":"0.3","title":"0.3","blurb":"0.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l0-3-1","title":"0.3.1 — Prefer wired headphones for playing and Capture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.3.1 — Prefer wired headphones for playing and Capture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.3 — Audio hygiene on iOS</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Plug in wired headphones before you play pads or Capture so the sound you hear matches the time you tapped."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton’s FAQ and the Bluetooth latency article give the same recommendation: use wired headphones to play the instruments in Note. Bluetooth and AirPods work, but wireless codecs add delay. That delay sits between your tap and the sound. Capture writes what you played in time with the transport. If you hear the pad late, you play late, and the clip inherits that lag."},{"type":"prose","html":"Wired headphones on these devices average about 5–10 milliseconds of latency. That is tight enough to Capture a groove. Bluetooth ranges from 34–200 milliseconds and can feel like a noticeable lag. Use a Lightning or USB-C headphone adapter if the iPhone or iPad has no headphone jack."},{"type":"prose","html":"This rule is strongest for playing and Capture. Listening back to a finished Demo Set on Bluetooth is less harmful because you are not performing. Still prefer wired when you judge timing. Speaker playback is not a documented latency figure in the extracts; treat it as a sketch speaker, not a Capture monitor."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pre-2.x Note can auto-monitor the audio input when headphones are connected (Lesson 0.3.3). That is another reason to wear wired cans when you sample: you hear the input without fighting Bluetooth delay on top of the mic."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Unpair or ignore Bluetooth headphones for this exercise.","Connect wired headphones (or a wired adapter + headphones).","Open a Set, tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> on a Drum Rack track, and play a four-on-the-floor kick while watching your finger.","Capture a short clip if you already know Capture; otherwise just play. Confirm the hit and the sound feel simultaneous. Write “wired for play/Capture” on your hygiene card."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Ableton recommend for playing Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Wired headphones.","Why not Bluetooth for Capture? <strong>Answer:</strong> 34–200 ms of extra latency makes you play late; Capture stores that late performance.","What latency do wired headphones typically add? <strong>Answer:</strong> About 5–10 ms."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Getting Started (“Can I use Bluetooth headphones or AirPods with Note?”)","Bluetooth Headphones and Latency in Note (wired 5–10 ms; recommendation to use an adapter)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play the same 8-bar pad pattern on wired headphones. Do not compare Bluetooth yet — that is 0.3.2. Circle “wired” on the hygiene card as the default for every Capture lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-3-2","title":"0.3.2 — Bluetooth headphone latency","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.3.2 — Bluetooth headphone latency","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.3 — Audio hygiene on iOS</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Measure the feel of Bluetooth lag against wired cans and write a personal latency policy."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Using wireless Bluetooth headphones with Note may delay both instrument playing and song playback. The delay is the time audio takes to travel to the headphones and be decoded — audio latency. Bluetooth codecs run higher latency than wired, from 34 to 200 milliseconds. You hear a gap between the tap and the sound. Wired averages 5–10 milliseconds."},{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ says this latency currently affects all audio applications, not only Note. Newer Bluetooth standards can improve the number, but you are limited by the adapter inside the phone or tablet. Note 1.0.12 added a Bluetooth delay warning with a “Don’t show again” option. If you dismissed it, the physics did not change."},{"type":"prose","html":"If lag seems worse than usual, unpair in iOS <strong>Settings → Bluetooth</strong>, forget the headphones, and pair again. That is Ableton’s check for abnormal Bluetooth behavior. It will not turn 200 ms into 5 ms."},{"type":"prose","html":"AirPods are Bluetooth headphones. They get the same range. Do not plan a Capture session on AirPods and then blame Note’s quantize. Use wired for playing and Capture; reserve Bluetooth for casual listen-back if you must."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Play a repeating 16th-note hat or kick on wired headphones for 20 seconds.","Switch to Bluetooth or AirPods (if you own them) and play the same pattern. Notice the gap.","Switch back to wired. Write the policy: “Wired for play/Capture. Bluetooth only for casual listen-back.”","If Bluetooth felt broken rather than merely late, unpair and re-pair, then repeat step 2."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What latency range does Ableton publish for Bluetooth in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> 34–200 ms.","Is the delay unique to Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The FAQ says Bluetooth latency currently affects all audio applications.","What is the first troubleshooting step for abnormal Bluetooth lag? <strong>Answer:</strong> iOS Settings → Bluetooth, delete the headphones, pair them again."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Bluetooth Headphones and Latency in Note (34–200 ms vs 5–10 ms; unpair/re-pair)","Note FAQ, Getting Started (Bluetooth / AirPods; recommend wired)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (Bluetooth delay warning)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a two-line policy in your notes and keep it next to the version sticky. You will need it every time a later lesson says “Capture.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-3-3","title":"0.3.3 — Monitor Audio Input preference (headphones) — pre-2.x settings location","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.3.3 — Monitor Audio Input preference (headphones) — pre-2.x settings location","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.3 — Audio hygiene on iOS</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 1.x, find Monitor Audio Input in Preferences and set it for headphone sampling."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson applies to Note versions before 2.0. Confirm <strong>App Settings → Help</strong> first. If you are on 2.0 or 2.0.1+, skip the toggle hunt and read Lesson 0.3.4. The pre-2.0 manual is the surface for this page."},{"type":"prose","html":"In Note 1.x, <strong>Monitor Audio Input</strong> lives in <strong>App Settings → Preferences → Other</strong>. Enable it to automatically monitor audio input when headphones are connected. You then hear the input signal while recording into the Drum or Melodic Sampler. Note 1.0.15 is the release that added “monitor audio input when headphones connected.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"The preference is global. It is not a per-track arm button. Headphones are the documented condition; the manual does not describe speaker monitoring for this toggle. Use wired headphones (0.3.1) so the monitored input is not stacked on Bluetooth delay."},{"type":"prose","html":"You still need the iOS microphone permission (0.2.2–0.2.3). Monitoring does not grant the mic; it only lets you hear the input Note is already allowed to use."},{"type":"prose","html":"If Help shows 2.x, this Settings toggle has been removed. Do not file a bug when you cannot find it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Read the version on your sticky. If it is 2.0+, stop and go to 0.3.4.","On 1.x, open <strong>App Settings → Preferences → Other</strong>.","Enable <strong>Monitor Audio Input</strong>. Connect wired headphones. Open a Drum Rack or Sampler and look at the record/mic control so you know where monitoring will apply later.","Recite the pre-2.x path from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is Monitor Audio Input on Note 1.x? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>App Settings → Preferences → Other</strong>.","When does it listen? <strong>Answer:</strong> It automatically monitors audio input when headphones are connected, for recording into Drum or Melodic Sampler.","Does this lesson apply on Note 2.0.1? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. 2.0.1 removed the Settings toggle. Use Lesson 0.3.4."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.5.2 Monitor Audio Input (pre-2.0 surface)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (monitor audio input when headphones connected)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (toggle removed — version gate)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write “1.x monitor = Preferences → Other” or “I am on 2.x — see 0.3.4” on the hygiene card. Do not record a long sample yet; this is only the location."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-3-4","title":"0.3.4 — Note 2.x: monitoring moves into track/set (release notes)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.3.4 — Note 2.x: monitoring moves into track/set (release notes)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.3 — Audio hygiene on iOS</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+, enable input monitoring on the track, not in App Settings."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 2.0.1 removed the <strong>Input Monitoring</strong> toggle from Settings. Monitoring is now managed from inside the Set. Audio inputs can be monitored and processed through audio tracks. You enable input monitoring from the audio track view or from the track context menu in Session View. It is exclusive and fixed to the track you enable it on. A track-icon indicator shows the input signal in real time."},{"type":"prose","html":"Empty Sampler view gained the same features as the empty audio clip. Input sources are selected from the built-in microphones and connected external devices, from the microphone button in the empty audio clip and Sampler views."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is a version gate. The printed manual still describes <strong>Monitor Audio Input</strong> under Preferences (Lesson 0.3.3). That paragraph is the pre-2.0 surface. If Help shows 2.0.1+, believe the release notes when the two disagree."},{"type":"prose","html":"Headphones still matter. Bluetooth latency (34–200 ms) is not fixed by moving the toggle. Use wired cans when you monitor an input you are about to record. The orange mic indicator remains expected while Note has the microphone."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The 2.0.1 notes do not publish a full click-by-click tree for every iPhone vs iPad layout of the track context menu. Use the two documented places: audio track view, and the track context menu in Session View."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Help shows 2.0 or 2.0.1+. If you are on 1.x, use 0.3.3 instead.","Open <strong>App Settings → Preferences</strong> and confirm <strong>Input Monitoring</strong> / <strong>Monitor Audio Input</strong> is gone.","Open a Set. Find an audio track (or the empty Sampler / empty audio clip). Enable monitoring from the track view or the Session track context menu. Watch the input indicator on the track icon.","Disable monitoring on that track. Recite: 2.x monitoring is per track inside the Set; Settings toggle removed."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where did the Settings monitoring toggle go in 2.0.1? <strong>Answer:</strong> It was removed. Monitoring is managed from within the Set.","Where do you enable it now? <strong>Answer:</strong> Audio track view, or the track context menu in Session View. Exclusive to that track.","Can you still choose built-in vs external inputs? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. From the microphone button in the empty audio clip and Sampler views."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1, Input Monitoring; Audio Interface Support","Ableton Note Manual §2.4.5.2 (pre-2.0 location — do not blend)","Bluetooth Headphones and Latency in Note (latency still applies)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On the hygiene card write your version and one line: “Monitor on track X, not in Settings.” Leave monitoring off when you are not sampling so you do not hear extra input on that track."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-3-5","title":"0.3.5 — The orange microphone indicator is expected","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.3.5 — The orange microphone indicator is expected","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.3 — Audio hygiene on iOS</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Treat the iOS orange microphone indicator as normal Note behavior, not a privacy failure."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ: when you are using Note, an orange microphone icon appears in iOS. That is expected. Note accesses the microphone of your device by default. The icon is iOS telling you an app is using the mic, not a warning that Note is secretly recording a Set to Cloud."},{"type":"prose","html":"You granted that access at first launch or in <strong>Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Microphone</strong>. Sampling, input monitoring (1.x preference or 2.x per-track), and the default mic path all keep the hardware available. The orange dot or pill can show even when you are only playing pads, because the FAQ describes default access, not “only while Record Sample is armed.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not a crash and not something to file as a bug. Do not revoke the mic permission just to hide the icon unless you are willing to lose sample recording. If you do revoke it, use Lesson 0.2.3 to restore access before any sampling lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"Cloud uploads are a different path (internet + Ableton account). The mic icon is local hardware status. Ableton’s privacy documents live under <strong>Privacy &amp; Legal</strong>; they do not say the orange icon means your sketch is being streamed."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Note and leave it in the foreground on the Sets Library or a Set.","Look at the iOS status area for the orange microphone indicator.","Confirm iOS Settings still lists Note under Microphone.","Write from memory: orange mic icon = expected; Note has default mic access."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Is the orange microphone icon a defect? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The FAQ says it is expected.","Why is it there? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note accesses the device microphone by default.","Will turning the mic off in iOS only hide the icon? <strong>Answer:</strong> It hides the icon by revoking access, which blocks sample recording. Re-grant via Privacy &amp; Security → Microphone if you still need to sample."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ, Reporting Problems (“When I’m using Note, an orange microphone icon appears…”)","Ableton Note Manual §1.1 Setup (microphone access)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Add “orange mic = expected” to the hygiene card. If a classmate asks whether Note is “always recording them,” answer with the FAQ sentence, then show the Privacy &amp; Security toggle."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-3-6","title":"0.3.6 — Note keeps the screen awake while it is in the foreground","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.3.6 — Note keeps the screen awake while it is in the foreground","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.3 — Audio hygiene on iOS</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Expect the display to stay on while Note is in front, and plan power around that fact."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.0.15 changed idle behavior: the screen no longer turns off when Note is in the foreground. You can play pads, watch a clip run, or leave a metronome click without the device auto-locking on top of the app. That is the documented behavior from the release notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"The extracts do not say the screen stays awake when Note is in the background or when another app is in front. {type:'gap'} Battery-percentage figures, Low Power Mode interactions, and whether iOS auto-lock still applies after you leave Note are not published in the Note manual, FAQ, or release notes. Use the device’s own Lock Screen and Low Power settings if you need the phone to sleep after you switch apps."},{"type":"prose","html":"Foreground-awake is useful for Capture and for watching scene launches. It is also how a phone on a music stand can drain while you think. Plug in power for long sessions. Wired headphones still apply (0.3.1). The orange mic icon can stay visible the whole time (0.3.5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not treat a sleeping screen as a Note crash. If the screen did sleep while Note was clearly in front on 1.0.15+, that is worth a reproducible report (0.2.8) with your version number."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm your version is 1.0.15 or later (Help).","Open a Set, start Play or launch a clip, and wait longer than your usual auto-lock time without touching the glass.","Confirm the screen stayed on. Stop playback.","Recite the rule: in the foreground, Note keeps the screen awake (1.0.15+)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When does Note keep the screen awake? <strong>Answer:</strong> While Note is in the foreground, as of Note 1.0.15.","Is that described in the printed Welcome chapter? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is a release-notes fact (1.0.15).","Does this lesson give you a battery-hour rating? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. {type:'gap'} Official extracts do not publish battery-life numbers."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (screen no longer turns off when Note is in the foreground)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write “foreground = screen stays on (1.0.15+)” on the hygiene card. For your next sketch session, plug the device in before you hit Play so a long Scene listen cannot kill the battery mid-idea."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-3-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-0-4-104","code":"0.4","title":"0.4","blurb":"0.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l0-4-1","title":"0.4.1 — Record first; evaluate later","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.1 — Record first; evaluate later","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Separate the session where you Capture from the session where you decide if the idea is good."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Beatmaking with Ableton Note</em> treats Note as a sketchbook for beats and song ideas, not as the place you finish a record. The article’s first judgment rule is space: store the raw idea, then put time between creating and judging. If you grade every bar while the transport is running, you stop playing. Capture needs the opposite habit."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note supports that split. Auto-save writes the Set every 10–15 seconds. <strong>+ New</strong> is cheap. You can fill clip slots and scenes now and refuse to open the mixer. Evaluation is a later listen — the article suggests a week or a month — or a listen with people who do not obsess over beats. This lesson only installs the split. Later 0.4 lessons add title, prompt, limitation, intention, and the “memorable after a delay” test."},{"type":"prose","html":"The four verbs stay in order. Sketch and Capture now. Sample if the room gives you something. Send when the idea has survived a delay. Do not mix, replace every preset, or hunt AUv3 during the first pass. Those jobs are either later or they are not Note’s jobs."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you already hate the loop at minute two, do not delete the Set. Duplicate it from <strong>Set Options</strong> and keep moving. Deleting is a judgment call. This lesson forbids that call until a later listen."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write on paper: “Today I only Capture. I do not rate the music.”","Tap <strong>+ New</strong>. Play pads on the first Drum Rack or melodic track for a few minutes. Capture at least one clip if you already know how; otherwise leave the improvisation in the buffer and add the clip.","Name the Set something temporary and close it. Do not open the mixer.","Recite the rule from memory: create now, judge after a delay."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When does the Beatmaking article tell you to judge an idea? <strong>Answer:</strong> After space — store the raw idea, then listen later (it names a week or a month).","Why is that rule useful in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Capture needs you to keep playing. Auto-save already keeps the Set.","What do you skip in the first session? <strong>Answer:</strong> Rating, heavy mixing, and deleting the idea because you dislike it in the moment."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How Can I Tell if My Beat Ideas are Any Good?”","Beatmaking with Ableton Note, Conclusion (separate creation from judgment)","Note FAQ (auto-save every 10–15 seconds)","Ableton Note Manual §3.1; §3.3 Set Options (duplicate)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep today’s Set closed for the rest of this module. You will reopen it only when a later lesson asks you to listen after a delay."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-2","title":"0.4.2 — Use a working title to constrain the sketch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.2 — Use a working title to constrain the sketch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Name the Set before you play so the title picks instruments and mood for you."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Beatmaking article starts inspiration with a song title. A title gives you an idea of what the beat might sound like and which instruments to choose. Its examples are playful on purpose: “Meta Balloons,” “Dinosaur Torpedo.” Neither title is a genre tag. Each one already implies texture, tempo feel, and palette."},{"type":"prose","html":"In Note you apply that by renaming the Set before the first clip. From the Sets Library, tap the three dots next to the play button and choose rename. A working title is not a marketing title. You can change it after the delay test (0.4.9–0.4.10). What you need now is a constraint so you do not open <strong>+ New</strong> and stare at three random Track Presets with no question to answer."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent a BPM table to match the words. The extracts do not assign tempos to titles. Let the title pick a drum kit versus a mallet, a busy hat versus a long reverb shot, then play. If the title is “Meta Balloons,” you might reach for a soft sampler and a floating chord. If it is “Dinosaur Torpedo,” you might reach for a heavy kick and a simple bass. Those are examples of thinking, not required presets."},{"type":"prose","html":"Eight tracks and eight scenes are enough to serve one title. They are not enough to serve five titles in one Set. One working title per Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write two candidate titles on paper. Circle one. Do not spend more than a minute.","Tap <strong>+ New</strong>, then <strong>Set Options</strong> (three dots) and rename the Set to that title.","Look at the three default tracks. Swap one Track Preset only if the title clearly disagrees with it. Play for five minutes inside that name.","Close the Set. Recite why you named it first: the title chooses sound and mood."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does a working title do, according to the article? <strong>Answer:</strong> It suggests how the music might sound and which instruments to choose.","Where do you put the title in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Rename the Set in <strong>Set Options</strong>.","Must the title stay forever? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is a working constraint. You can rename after you decide the idea is worth finishing."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How to Get Started Sketching Beat Ideas” (A Song Title)","Ableton Note Manual §3.3 Set Options (rename)","Ableton Note Manual §3.1 (default tracks / Track Presets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep a running list of unused titles in your notes (“Meta Balloons” style). Next idle moment, pick one and make a Set that only answers that name."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-3","title":"0.4.3 — Use a written prompt to start a sketch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.3 — Use a written prompt to start a sketch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write one prompt on paper and let that sentence start the first clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If a title is a name, a prompt is a direction. The Beatmaking article: writing with a prompt gives you some direction. Its examples: “Write a song about what video games will look like in the future.” “Write a song about New York.” You are not scoring a film. You are giving the next ten minutes a subject so the pads have something to be about."},{"type":"prose","html":"Write the prompt before <strong>+ New</strong>. Put it in the Set name, or keep it on paper next to the device. A prompt can live beside a working title (“Dinosaur Torpedo” + “write a song about New York”) or replace the title for one session. Do not stack five prompts. One sentence is the constraint."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note does not have a prompt field. {type:'gap'} The app has no documented lyrics pad or prompt template. The writing happens outside the UI, then you sketch on the Session grid. Use clips and later scenes to answer the sentence: one scene as the “hook” of the prompt, another as the sparser “story” pass. That mapping is 0.4.8. Today you only need the sentence and one loop that could belong to it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Stay inside Note’s limits. Eight tracks, sixteen-bar clips. A prompt about a city does not unlock Arrangement View."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write one prompt in a single sentence. Use the article’s style, not a production checklist.","Create a Set. Optionally put a short form of the prompt in the Set name.","Capture or play one clip that could only exist because of that sentence (a rhythm, a pad color, a bass interval).","Read the prompt aloud, then play the clip. If they do not meet, change the music, not the sentence, for this exercise."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does a prompt give you? <strong>Answer:</strong> Direction — a subject to write toward, such as a place or an image of the future.","Where do you store the prompt? <strong>Answer:</strong> On paper or in the Set name. Note has no dedicated prompt screen.","How many prompts belong in one session? <strong>Answer:</strong> One. Extra prompts are new Sets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How to Get Started Sketching Beat Ideas” (A Prompt)","Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View (clips/scenes as the place the answer lives)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Save three unused prompts in your notes. The next time you stall on a blank Set, pick one and start before you browse presets."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-4","title":"0.4.4 — Optional constraints for a session","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.4 — Optional constraints for a session","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pick one artificial limitation and finish a short sketch that obeys it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Beatmaking article’s third starter is a limitation. Examples: “I’m going to write a beat with no bass line.” “I’m only going to use these 2 notes.” Limitations give a busy mind a challenge to focus on. They are optional, but they are more useful than a blank eight-track grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"Choose limits that Note can enforce. Two notes: turn <strong>In Key</strong> on or simply refuse every pad outside those two pitches. No bass: do not add a bass Track Preset; leave that column empty. One Drum Rack only: swipe left, see <strong>Add Track</strong>, and do not tap it. Sixteen bars is already a product cap — you may also choose 2 or 4 bars as a tighter loop. Do not invent device limits the FAQ does not state."},{"type":"prose","html":"A limitation is not a moral rule for every Set. It is a session tool. Combine it with a title or a prompt if you want, but if the session feels heavy, keep only the limitation. The article’s point is focus, not punishment."},{"type":"prose","html":"When the time is up, do not “fix” the limit by adding the missing bass. That is a new session with a new rule. Duplicate the Set if you want a version with bass later."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write one limitation: no bass, two notes, drums only, or one scene.","Open <strong>+ New</strong>. If the limit is “no bass,” ignore or delete the bass-style default track (long-press → Delete) so you cannot cheat.","Play and Capture inside the limit for about eight minutes.","Stop. State the limitation from memory and whether you broke it. If you broke it, duplicate and try once more."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What are the article’s example limitations? <strong>Answer:</strong> A beat with no bass line; a beat that uses only two notes.","Why use a limitation? <strong>Answer:</strong> It gives a busy mind a challenge to focus on.","May you add the missing part at the end of the same timed pass? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Changing the rule is a new session. Duplicate the Set if you want a second version."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How to Get Started Sketching Beat Ideas” (A Limitation)","Ableton Note Manual §4.1 Tracks (add / long-press delete); §5.3 Key &amp; Scale","Note FAQ (8 tracks, 16-bar clips)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a tiny menu of limits you like. Circle one default for Level 1 lab Sets so you never start with “anything is allowed.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-5","title":"0.4.5 — State a session intention before playing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.5 — State a session intention before playing","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Say one intention out loud, take a breath, then touch the pads."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Beatmaking article pairs two resets: an intention, and deep breaths. Set an intention for what you are about to do; you are programming yourself for success. Then bring mental focus to your breathing and calm your mind before you begin. Neither step is a Note control. Both happen before <strong>+ New</strong> or before you tap <strong>Add Clip</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"An intention is not a title and not a prompt. A title names the piece. A prompt gives it a subject. An intention names the job: “I will Capture a two-bar kick and hat,” “I will record one room sample,” “I will send yesterday’s Set to Cloud.” Keep it small enough to finish inside Note’s eight-track, eight-scene box. “I will finish the album” is not a session intention."},{"type":"prose","html":"Breathe once slowly so you do not open the mixer to soothe nerves. The article puts breath before writing a beat, not after you already hate the loop. If you skip this and dive into presets, you are browsing, not intending."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can write the intention on the same card as the working title. Read it, breathe, then play. When the intention is done, stop even if time remains. Extra time is how 0.4.6’s polish spiral starts."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write one intention in one sentence. Example: “Capture a drum loop and leave.”","Sit with the device locked. Take three slow breaths.","Unlock, open or create the Set, and do only that intention.","Stop. Repeat the intention from memory and mark done or not done. No extra tracks."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does the article say an intention does? <strong>Answer:</strong> You set an intention for what you are about to do — programming yourself for success.","What accompanies it? <strong>Answer:</strong> Deep breaths to bring focus to breathing and calm the mind before you begin.","How is an intention different from a prompt? <strong>Answer:</strong> A prompt is the subject of the music. An intention is the job of this sitting."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How to Get Started Sketching Beat Ideas” (An Intention; Deep Breaths)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write tomorrow’s intention tonight. Make it smaller than you like. That sentence is the first thing you read when you open Note."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-6","title":"0.4.6 — Finish the musical idea before mixing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.6 — Finish the musical idea before mixing","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture the main parts of a sketch before you open the mixer or chase presets."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Beatmaking article invokes the 80/20 rule: you will probably come up with the main parts of the beat very quickly. Do not spend too much time fine-tuning an idea until you decide it is worth continuing. That decision happens after a delay (0.4.9), not while the first loop is still warm."},{"type":"prose","html":"In Note the temptation is the mixer (<strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong>, <strong>M</strong>, <strong>S</strong>, volume sliders) and the two effect tabs on every Track Preset. Those controls are real and later lessons use them. They are not the first job. The first job is drums, bass, harmony, and a lead or hook clip — the parts in 0.4.7 — sitting in clip slots you can launch. Fine-tuning Reverb, Saturator, or Main FX before those parts exist is polish on silence."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note is not the desktop mix course. Sister course <strong>Foundations of Music Production</strong> and <strong>Mastering Ableton Live 12</strong> own full mix architecture. Here you may set levels so you can hear the idea. You do not start a mix spiral: solo-hopping, endless EQ, or replacing every preset. The article’s conclusion again: separate creation of the idea from judgment of it."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you notice you have been on one hat sample for ten minutes, you are already past 80/20. Duplicate the clip if you want a safer copy, then move to the next part."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write “parts first, mix later” on the session card.","In a new or current Set, Capture or place clips for at least two parts (for example kick pattern + a bass or chord). Do not open the mixer until both clips play together.","Open <strong>Show/Hide Mixer</strong> only long enough to make both parts audible. Close the mixer.","Recite 80/20: main parts come quickly; do not fine-tune until the idea earns a later session."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the 80/20 warning in the article? <strong>Answer:</strong> The main parts arrive quickly. Do not spend too long fine-tuning until you decide the idea is worth continuing.","What counts as “the idea” in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Launchable clips for the main parts — not a finished mix.","When do you decide it is worth continuing? <strong>Answer:</strong> After a delay, not during the first Capture pass."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How Can I Tell if My Beat Ideas are Any Good?” (80/20)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note, Conclusion","Ableton Note Manual §4.4 Mixer","Course syllabus sister-course boundaries (Production / Live own full mix)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set a phone timer for eight minutes. Add parts only. When it rings, close Note even if a filter sweep is unfinished."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-7","title":"0.4.7 — Parts of a sketch: drums, bass, harmony, lead","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.7 — Parts of a sketch: drums, bass, harmony, lead","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Name the usual sketch parts and park each one on its own Note track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Beatmaking article lists tried-and-true beat parts. Kick: punchy pulse, usually on the downbeat, working with the bass. 808/bass: a tuned low line that sets the root. Snare: the backbeat answer to the kick. Hi-hat: motion and note-division story. Chord progression: three or four chords looped until they bore you, then changed; delay and reverb help them linger. Shots: a vocal phrase, reverse cymbal, or long-reverb piano so the loop does not go stale. The hook/chorus should have a memorable melody and rhythm — that is the lead you protect."},{"type":"prose","html":"Map those jobs onto Note’s eight tracks without pretending you need all eight. A compact sketch is: Drum Rack (kick, snare, hat on pads), a bass or 808-style preset, a harmonic preset (chords), a lead or hook line, optional shot. Default <strong>+ New</strong> Sets already preload drum, bass, and melodic or Sampler tracks. Use that gift. Long-press a track to duplicate if you need a second Drum Rack for shots."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not the Drums and Rhythm Programming course. You are placing parts so the sketch is listenable, not designing a full groove curriculum. You are also not required to use an 808; the article tells the history so you understand tuned bass. Any bass preset that states the root counts."},{"type":"prose","html":"Stay inside 16-bar clips. A four-bar loop of these parts is enough to test the idea."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On paper, list four lanes: drums, bass, harmony, lead. Add shots as optional.","Open a Set. Assign one track to each lane. If a default track already matches, keep it.","Play a kick on the downbeat, a snare on the backbeat, a bass root, and a short chord or lead figure. Capture what you can.","Launch the clips together. From memory, name which track is which part."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where does the kick usually sit, according to the article? <strong>Answer:</strong> On the downbeat, working with the bass for motion and low-frequency push.","What makes a chorus/hook worth keeping? <strong>Answer:</strong> A memorable melody and rhythm (and maybe a temporary title or lyrics).","Must you fill all eight tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Eight is the maximum. A sketch can be drums + bass + harmony + lead."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “Which Instruments Should I Use for My Beat Ideas?”","Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How Many Song Sections Should I Create?” (chorus/hook)","Ableton Note Manual §3.1 (default drum / bass / melodic / Sampler tracks); §4.1 (max 8 tracks)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On your session card, tick which of the four parts exist in your current Set. The empty boxes are the next Capture, not a reason to mix."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-8","title":"0.4.8 — Sections as future scenes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.8 — Sections as future scenes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Plan chorus, verse, and intro/outro as separate scenes instead of one endless loop."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Beatmaking article warns you not to stop at an amazing 8-bar loop that spins forever — though that loop is a great place to start. Then it names sections. Chorus/hook first: memorable melody and rhythm, maybe a temporary title or lyrics. Verse: the storytelling section, often sparser so a vocal can sit. Intro/outro: how you get in and out; try introducing a new instrument every 1, 2, or 4 bars, and keep it short."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note’s structure for that plan is scenes. A scene is one horizontal row of clips. Tap the scene number to launch every clip in the row. Each Set can have up to eight scenes. <strong>Show/Hide Scene Numbers</strong> reveals the column. Long-press a scene number to duplicate a row you like and then thin it for a verse."},{"type":"prose","html":"You are not arranging a timeline. There is no Arrangement View. You are stocking up to eight alternative rows you will later fire by hand or export top-to-bottom (export uses scene order, longest clip in each scene sets that scene’s length). Full song architecture still belongs to the Production sister course and to Live."},{"type":"prose","html":"Start with one loop in scene 1. Duplicate to scene 2 and remove parts for a verse. Scene 3 can be the hook with an extra lead. That is already more form than an infinite 8-bar cycle."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Show scene numbers. Play whatever loop you have in the first row.","Long-press the scene number and <strong>Duplicate</strong>. In the copy, stop or delete one track’s clip so the row is sparser (verse).","If you have a spare clip, put a fuller hook row in another scene. Tap scene numbers to hear the contrast.","From memory, name three section types (chorus, verse, intro/outro) and the Note object that holds them (scenes, max 8)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the article’s warning about a single loop? <strong>Answer:</strong> Avoid only having an amazing 8-bar loop that spins endlessly — but it is a good start.","How do you launch a whole section in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap the scene number to launch all clips in that row.","How many scenes can one Set hold? <strong>Answer:</strong> Eight."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How Many Song Sections Should I Create?”","Ableton Note Manual §4.3 Scenes (row of clips, max eight, launch by number)","Ableton Note Manual §10.2 Export Audio (scenes top to bottom)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a three-row map: Scene 1 hook, Scene 2 verse (fewer parts), Scene 3 intro or outro. Build at least two of those rows in the current Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-9","title":"0.4.9 — Evaluate a sketch after a delay","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.9 — Evaluate a sketch after a delay","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Park a raw Set, wait, then listen once before you edit."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The article’s test for “is this any good?” starts with storage and time. Find a place to store raw ideas. Give yourself space between when you create and when you judge or edit. Specifically: put it away and listen later. Give it a week or a month and see if you still love it. Organize Sets in a folder and rate them, or separate them (the article jokes “The Good, The Bad and the Ugly”). Ask people who do not obsess about beats which ideas they like."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note’s local storage is Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → Projects. Cloud is not that folder and is not a backup. Eight Cloud slots are for transfer. Keep raw sketches Local until you decide one should go to Live. Rename clearly so “Dinosaur Torpedo raw” is not overwritten by a polish pass; <strong>Duplicate</strong> first if you must keep working."},{"type":"prose","html":"This lesson is the delay, not the mix. When you return, play the Set from the library preview or open it and launch scenes. Do not immediately swap every preset. Mark keep / maybe / drop on paper. A drop still stays on disk until you have a backup if you ever delete (Level 1)."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Ableton does not publish a required wait time. The week-or-month figures come from the Beatmaking article, not from the Note manual. Use any delay long enough that you are no longer performing the take in your head."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Pick a Set you already sketched in 0.4. Close it. Write today’s date and “do not open until ____” (at least overnight; a week if you can).","Confirm it sits in the Local library (or note if it is already in Cloud).","When the date arrives, preview or open the Set once, launch scenes, and write keep / maybe / drop. Do not edit in that listen.","Recite the rule: store raw, wait, then judge."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What delay does the article suggest? <strong>Answer:</strong> Put it away and listen later — a week or a month.","Where do local Note projects live? <strong>Answer:</strong> Files → On My iPhone/iPad → Ableton Note → Projects. Cloud is not the backup.","What do you do on the return listen? <strong>Answer:</strong> Listen and rate. You do not begin a mix in the same motion."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How Can I Tell if My Beat Ideas are Any Good?” (Time; Organize; Ask Randos)","Note FAQ, Managing Files in Note (local path; Cloud is not backup)","Ableton Note Manual §3.2 Sets Library; §3.3 Duplicate / Rename"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Put a calendar reminder for the return listen. Until it fires, work only on new Sets or on lessons that do not edit that sketch."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-10","title":"0.4.10 — Finish ideas that remain memorable after a delay","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.10 — Finish ideas that remain memorable after a delay","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Send or develop only the sketch that still sticks in your head after you waited."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Beatmaking article’s sharpest filter is memory: if one of your beat ideas is stuck in your head all the time, that is the one you should focus on. Combined with the delay (0.4.9) and 80/20 (0.4.6), you now have a finish rule. Do not finish every Set. Finish the one that remains memorable after you were not looking at it."},{"type":"prose","html":"“Finish” in this course still means a Note finish: scenes that state hook vs verse, parts in place, then Cloud or <strong>Share To…</strong> or <strong>Export Audio</strong> toward Live. It does not mean a mastered album inside eight tracks. Sister courses take the long form. The article’s own close: sketching lets you make music without the pressure of completing full songs. Separating creation from judgment shows you which ideas are worth finishing."},{"type":"prose","html":"If nothing is stuck in your head, that is data. Start another titled, prompted, limited session. Do not spend a week polishing a loop you cannot hum. If two ideas compete, play them for someone who does not obsess over beats and notice which one they repeat back."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the losers. Organize rather than delete. Today’s “ugly” folder is tomorrow’s shot or hat pattern."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["After at least one delayed listen, write yes/no: “This is still in my head.”","If yes, duplicate the Set, give the copy a name that includes Finish, and add only what the hook still needs (a scene or a lead clip).","If no, leave the Set in Local and start a new <strong>+ New</strong> with a fresh title.","Recite the filter: memorable after a delay = worth finishing; otherwise sketch again."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which idea should you focus on? <strong>Answer:</strong> The one that stays stuck in your head.","Does finishing mean completing a full song in Note? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The article treats sketching as relief from that pressure. Finish enough to send or to develop the hook.","What happens to ideas that fail the test? <strong>Answer:</strong> Keep them organized. Do not polish them first."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note, “How Can I Tell if My Beat Ideas are Any Good?” (Memorable; 80/20; Time)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note, Conclusion","Ableton Note Manual §10 (send via Cloud, Share, or export)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"List your last three Sets. Mark each memorable / not. Circle one finish candidate. That circle is the only Set you are allowed to upload in the next Cloud lesson unless a teacher assigns otherwise."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l0-4-11","title":"0.4.11 — Session plan: time limit and mixing limit","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"0.4.11 — Session plan: time limit and mixing limit","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 0 · Module 0.4 — Mobile production mindset</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a one-page sketch contract you will follow before Level 1: a time box and a stop-before-mix rule."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Level 0.4 ends with a contract, not a new Note control. The Beatmaking article already gave the clauses. Start with title, prompt, limitation, or intention (and a breath). Capture parts first (drums, bass, harmony, lead; scenes as sections). Do not fine-tune until a delayed listen says the idea is memorable. Those sentences are the contract. You add a clock so the 80/20 rule has a boundary."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Neither the Note manual nor the Beatmaking article publishes an official session length (no 25- or 45-minute Ableton standard). You choose the time box. Pick a span short enough that you cannot start a mix spiral — often one sitting on a couch or a ride with wired headphones. Write the number yourself."},{"type":"prose","html":"The mixing limit is sourced: do not spend too much time fine-tuning until you decide the idea is worth continuing, and separate creation from judgment. In Note that means: mixer only to hear parts; no Main FX rabbit hole; no preset shopping after the parts exist. When the timer ends, close the app. Foreground-awake (1.0.15+) will not stop you; you have to stop."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the contract with your version sticky. You will reuse it for every lab in later levels."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Copy this skeleton into your notes and fill the blanks:","Working title:","Prompt (optional):","Limitation (optional):","Intention:","Time box (your number):","Parts I will Capture (drums / bass / harmony / lead / shots):","Scenes I will stock (max 8):","Stop rule: no mix, no delayed-judgment during the clock","After: close Note; wait before rating","Run that contract once now for a short sitting.","When the time box ends, stop playback and leave the library.","Recite your stop rule from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Who sets the session length? <strong>Answer:</strong> You do. Official extracts do not fix a number.","What is the mixing limit? <strong>Answer:</strong> Do not fine-tune or begin a full mix until a later listen says the idea is worth continuing.","What four starters can the contract borrow from the article? <strong>Answer:</strong> Title, prompt, limitation, intention (plus breath)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Beatmaking with Ableton Note (title, prompt, limitation, intention, breath, 80/20, parts, sections, delay, memorable)","Ableton Note Manual §4 Session View / Mixer (where you refuse to linger)","Note FAQ (8 tracks, 8 scenes, 16-bar clips)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (screen stays awake — you still end the session)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Photograph the filled contract. That photo is the Level 0 checkpoint. Do not start Level 1 until it exists."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l0-4-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
